# Drive base_link to a pose that keeps moving: a tracked pose, updated from a
# topic or a TF frame for the whole goal, plus a fixed offset. Served by
# controller_server (syncai_controller, SyncAI-Robot-Workspace) on the
# relative name `track_pose` -> <robot_id>/track_pose, beside follow_path and
# dock_to_pose, one of the three at a time. New 2026-11, for the last metre of
# a charge: a node of the charge tree polls the landmark detector's
# detect_landmark service and publishes the sightings it trusts on the topic
# this follows; the detector itself publishes nothing. Nothing in this action
# knows what is tracked.
#
# Modelled on nav2's FollowObject (opennav_following), whose field names it
# keeps, with three differences, which are why it is not FollowObject itself
# (that one is also not a Humble apt package):
# - It SUCCEEDS once the goal checker accepts the pose. FollowObject stops
#   there and waits for the object to move again, and succeeds only after
#   its static_object_timeout or max_duration; a charge goes on to lie down.
# - The offset and the controller / goal checker are per goal. FollowObject's
#   distance is one server parameter along the object's heading; chargers of
#   different models dock at different offsets, and this stack picks plugins
#   per goal (DockToPose does).
# - Its codes sit in NavigateToGoal's 3xx docking range (FollowObject: 9xx).
#
# Why not DockToPose with its goal re-sent: DockToPose treats a moved target
# as a hazard -- every preempt restarts its timeout and re-checks that the
# robot stands in front of the new pose, which keeps dock.xml from driving
# blind to a dock moved after staging -- and its goal is planar, with no
# stamp, transformed with the TF of the moment it arrives. Tracking needs the
# opposite on both counts.
#
# Each tracked pose is transformed into the controller's odom frame AT ITS OWN
# STAMP (the capture time: the robot has moved since), the offset applied,
# and the result projected onto the floor (x, y, yaw) as the target the
# controller plugin is handed. No path, no costmap: like dock_to_pose, the
# approach is blind, so a stale pose stops the robot (pose_timeout) and a
# lost one ends the goal (lost_timeout).

# Topic of geometry_msgs/PoseStamped to follow, any frame, stamped with when
# the pose was true. Every message is trusted: a publisher unsure of a pose
# does not publish it (the charge tree drops a DetectLandmark answer that is
# ambiguous), rather than leave the judgement to a controller that cannot make
# it.
string pose_topic
# Or a TF frame to follow, when pose_topic is empty. Exactly one of the two.
string tracked_frame
# Where base_link must end up, in the tracked pose's own frame. For a charger
# tag (+x out of its face, +z up) docked 0.33 m in front of it, facing it:
# position.x 0.33, orientation a yaw of pi. Only its planar part (x, y, yaw)
# survives the projection onto the floor.
geometry_msgs/Pose target_offset
# Id in controller_server's controller_plugins; empty -> the server's
# default_track_controller_id.
string controller_id
# Id in controller_server's goal_checker_plugins; empty -> the server's
# default_track_goal_checker_id.
string goal_checker_id
# No new pose this long: the robot stops and waits (feedback HOLDING), and
# moves again on the next pose. Zero -> the server's default.
builtin_interfaces/Duration pose_timeout
# No pose this long, from the start or since the last one: LOST. Zero -> the
# server's default.
builtin_interfaces/Duration lost_timeout
# The whole goal, from acceptance; tracked-pose updates never restart it.
# Zero -> the server's default.
builtin_interfaces/Duration max_duration
---
# Why the goal ended. Meaningful ONLY with status ABORTED; SUCCEEDED and
# CANCELED carry NONE, and ABORTED + NONE is "superseded or not started" (the
# server aborts a preempted goal with a default result), as for DockToPose
# and NavigateToGoal.
#
# 33x: inside NavigateToGoal.DOCK_FAILED (300), as base + n, apart from
# DockToPose's 301-305 so a log line names the server. Never renumber.
uint16 NONE=0
uint16 UNKNOWN=1
# Both or neither of pose_topic / tracked_frame, or a target_offset that is
# not a rotation (a zero quaternion).
uint16 INVALID_GOAL=331
# A tracked pose could not be transformed into odom at its stamp (an unknown
# frame, a stamp older than the TF buffer), or the robot's own pose was
# unavailable.
uint16 TRANSFORM_FAILED=332
# No pose for lost_timeout: none ever came, or none since the last one.
uint16 LOST=333
# The controller threw, the robot pose was lost past failure_tolerance, or
# the progress checker saw no movement: error_msg has the controller's words.
uint16 FAILED_TO_CONTROL=334
# max_duration elapsed before the goal checker accepted the pose.
uint16 TIMEOUT=335
# follow_path or dock_to_pose was driving the robot.
uint16 CONTROLLER_BUSY=336
# controller_id or goal_checker_id names a plugin that is not loaded;
# error_msg lists the ones that are.
uint16 INVALID_PLUGIN=337

uint16 error_code
# Human-readable detail for the operator; empty when error_code is NONE.
string error_msg
builtin_interfaces/Duration total_elapsed_time
# Robot to target on the last control cycle that had both, whatever the
# status: metres and radians, lateral across the target's heading (+ = the
# robot is left of it). final_distance_error is -1.0 when no cycle had both
# (no pose ever came), and the other two are then meaningless.
float32 final_distance_error
float32 final_lateral_error
float32 final_yaw_error
---
# Published once per control cycle (controller_frequency).
uint8 WAITING_FOR_POSE=1
uint8 TRACKING=2
# The newest pose is older than pose_timeout: stopped, waiting for the next.
uint8 HOLDING=3
uint8 state
# Robot to target (0 while WAITING_FOR_POSE): metres and radians, lateral as
# in the result.
float32 distance_to_target
float32 lateral_error
float32 yaw_error
# How old the newest tracked pose is [s]; -1 while WAITING_FOR_POSE.
float32 pose_age
uint32 poses_received
builtin_interfaces/Duration tracking_time
